# The Vijayanagara and Bahmani Kingdoms

> When the Tughlaqs loosened their grip on the Deccan, two powers
> arose facing each other across the Tungabhadra and the Raichur
> doab: the Hindu Vijayanagara of 1336 and the Bahmani sultanate of
> 1347. For two centuries their wars, markets, and courts — painted
> for us by Persian and Portuguese travellers — made the Deccan one
> of the medieval world's liveliest crossroads.

## 1. Two States for the Deccan

Muhammad bin Tughlaq's over-extended Deccan experiments bred revolt.
In 1347 the Afghan officer **Ala-ud-din Hasan Bahman Shah** (Hasan
Gangu) seized the Deccan sultanate from Delhi and ruled from
**Gulbarga (Ahsanabad)**. Eleven years earlier, two brothers of the
Kampili country — **Harihara and Bukka** of the Sangama family — had
founded a city on the south bank of the Tungabhadra:
**Vijayanagara**, the "City of Victory".

[info title="The Vidyaranya tradition"]
Court tradition says Harihara and Bukka were captured by Muhammad
bin Tughlaq, converted to Islam, sent back as governors, and
reconverted to Hinduism by the sage **Vidyaranya** of Sringeri.
Whatever its exact details, the story shows the founders building a
state from the Tughlaq wreckage rather than against a living enemy.
[/info]

| Fact | Vijayanagara | Bahmani |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Founded | 1336, Harihara I and Bukka I | 1347, Ala-ud-din Hasan Bahman Shah |
| First capital | Vijayanagara (Hampi) | Gulbarga, then Bidar (c. 1424) |
| Core rivalry | Raichur doab | Krishna-Tungabhadra line |
| Court language | Kannada, Telugu, Sanskrit | Persian, Dakhni Urdu |

## 2. Vijayanagara: The Sangama Dynasty (1336-1485)

**Harihara I** and **Bukka I** consolidated Tungabhadra country;
Bukka's son **Kampana**, whose wife **Gangadevi** wrote the
*Madhura Vijayam*, destroyed the Madurai sultanate about 1371.
**Harihara II** (1377-1404) pushed to the Krishna. **Deva Raya I**
(1406-1422) warred with Bahmani **Firuz Shah** — losing Bankapur
and giving a daughter in marriage, then winning the long war of
Pangal — and built a dam and canals on the Tungabhadra.

**Deva Raya II** (1424-1446), styled *Gajabetekara* ("the elephant
hunter"), recruited Muslim archers with jagirs and drilled the army
for cavalry war. He honoured the Telugu poet **Srinatha** with a
kanakabhisheka (a shower of gold). The dynasty's last rulers were
weak; in 1485 the minister **Saluva Narasimha** seized the throne —
the second dynasty — and his regent Narasa Nayaka passed power to
the **Tuluva** house.

## 3. The Great Travellers' Testimony

| Traveller | From | About | What he saw |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Nicolo Conti | Venice | c. 1420 | Grandeur of Vijayanagara under Deva Raya I |
| Abdur Razzaq | Herat (Persia) | 1443 | Envoy of Shah Rukh; city "such that the eye has not seen" |
| Athanasius Nikitin | Russia | c. 1470 | Bahmani court and trade |
| Duarte Barbosa | Portugal | c. 1518 | Markets, horse trade, and officials |
| Domingo Paes | Portugal | c. 1520 | Hampi under Krishnadevaraya: "best-provided city in the world" |
| Fernao Nuniz | Portugal | c. 1535 | History, festivals, and wrestlers of the raya |

## 4. Krishnadevaraya and the Tuluva Zenith

**Krishnadevaraya** (1509-1529) is Vijayanagara's golden age. He
crushed the Bahmani army, took Gulbarga and Bidar and briefly
restored the Bahmani sultan; at **Raichur (1520)** he shattered
Ismail Adil Shah of Bijapur — Domingo Paes watched the battle. He
captured **Kondavidu (1515)** from the Gajapati king Prataparudra
of Orissa and sealed peace by marrying a Gajapati princess.

- He wrote the Telugu classic **Amuktamalyada** on devotion and
  kingship, with its famous chapter of statecraft: a king must
  always survey, irrigate, and defend.
- His court of **Ashtadiggajas** ("eight elephants" bearing the
  earth of letters) included **Allasani Peddana**, the "father of
  Telugu poetry", and **Tenali Ramakrishna**.
- He expanded the **Vitthala temple**, built the Krishna temple for
  the Balakrishna image taken from Udayagiri, and dug tanks and
  canals in the Rayalaseema country.

After him, Achyuta Raya (1529-1542) and the boy Sadasiva (1542-1570)
reigned while the minister **Rama Raya** of the Aravidu family
played the five Deccan sultanates against each other — until they
united against him.

@chart bar title="Duration of Vijayanagara's four dynasties (years)" labels:
Sangama (1336-1485)
Saluva (1485-1505)
Tuluva (1505-1570)
Aravidu (1570-1646)

