[warning title="Talikota, 23 January 1565"]
At **Rakshasa-Tangadi (Talikota)** the allied armies of Bijapur,
Ahmadnagar, and Golconda destroyed Rama Raya's host; the aged
minister was captured and beheaded by Hussain Nizam Shah. The
victors sacked Hampi for months; the capital was never rebuilt.
The Aravidu rajas carried on from Penugonda and Chandragiri until
about 1646 — at Chandragiri, Venkata III's officer granted the
English the site of Madras in 1639.
[/warning]

## 5. The Bahmani Kingdom: Gulbarga to Bidar

Ala-ud-din Hasan divided the kingdom into **four tarafs**
(provinces) — Gulbarga, Daulatabad, Bidar, and Berar — each with a
governor and garrison. **Firuz Shah Bahmani** (1397-1422), the
learned sultan who married Deva Raya I's daughter, built Firozabad,
studied astronomy, and filled his court with Persian scholars. His
brother **Ahmad Shah Wali** (1422-1436) shifted the capital to
**Bidar** and devastated Vijayanagara in turn.

Bahmani politics revolved round two noble factions: the **Deccanis**
(older settlers) and the **Afaqis** (recent Persian and Arab
arrivals) — a rivalry that eventually killed the state's ablest
statesman.

## 6. Mahmud Gawan: Reform and Tragedy

**Mahmud Gawan**, a Persian merchant of Gilan who became wazir to
Muhammad Shah III (1463-1481), conquered **Goa** from Vijayanagara
(1472), raided Kanchipuram (1481), and reformed the state: he
raised the tarafs from four to **eight**, enlarged the crown lands
(khalisa), tightened audits, and moved nobles and troops from
revenue assignments to **cash salaries** — to curb corruption and
private armies.

[important title="The forged letter"]
The Deccani faction, nursing its wounded privileges, forged a
letter implicating Gawan with the enemy; the aged wazir — then
about seventy-three — was executed in 1481. "The king has killed
the camel that carried his load," said the court. Within a
generation the Bahmani realm broke apart into five sultanates:
**Berar** (Imad Shahi), **Bijapur** (Adil Shahi), **Ahmadnagar**
(Nizam Shahi), **Golconda** (Qutb Shahi), and **Bidar** (Barid
Shahi).
[/important]

@diagram tree id="deccan-sultanates" layout="tree" aria="The five sultanates that replaced the Bahmani kingdom"
  Bahmani kingdom -> "Berar: Imad Shahi (c. 1490)"
  Bahmani kingdom -> "Bijapur: Adil Shahi (c. 1489)"
  Bahmani kingdom -> "Ahmadnagar: Nizam Shahi (1490)"
  Bahmani kingdom -> "Golconda: Qutb Shahi (c. 1512-1518)"
  Bahmani kingdom -> "Bidar: Barid Shahi (c. 1491)"
@/diagram

## 7. Administration, Economy, and Society

Vijayanagara's administration mixed old and new. The king (raya)
ruled with a council; provinces passed to princes and chiefs.
Under the **nayankara system**, military chiefs (**nayakas**) held
**amaram** — revenue assignments of land — on condition of paying
troops to the raya. Every village kept its hereditary **ayagars**:
headman, accountant, watchman, and the rest.

| Feature | Vijayanagara | Bahmani |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Military land grant | Amaram to nayakas | Jagirs and taraf governorships |
| Revenue demand | About one-sixth to one-third | Persian-style khalisa and iqta rates |
| Army backbone | Cavalry from Arabia, horses via Portuguese | Afghan-Turkic cavalry, artillery |
| Coins | Gold varaha (pagoda), silver fanam | Tanka and later sultanate coinage |

The economy glittered: Hampi's bazaars sold pearls by the measure
and muslins by the bale; **Golconda's diamond mines** (Kollur on
the Krishna) supplied the world; good war-horses were imported
through Bhatkal and, after 1510, Portuguese Goa. The **Dasara
(Mahanavami) festival** displayed the army and collected tribute;
women traded, danced, wrestled, and sometimes ruled villages,
Paes and Nuniz report with astonishment.

## 8. Architecture and the Indo-Islamic Synthesis

Hampi — a UNESCO World Heritage Site — shows both traditions
learning from each other. The **Virupaksha temple** grew from
pre-Vijayanagara shrines; the **Vitthala temple** holds the stone
chariot and halls of musical pillars; the royal chapel
**Hazara Rama** carries Ramayana friezes in stone. The style's
signature is the towering **rayagopuram**, the pillared
**kalyana mandapa**, and composite **yali** (lion-demon) pillars —
alongside the secular **Lotus Mahal**, Elephant Stables, and the
Mahanavami Dibba platform, built with arches and domes in Indo-
Islamic manner.

[definition]
**Dakhni Urdu** — the Deccan's own Persian-inflected Hindavi,
nurtured at Bahmani and sultanate courts. Poets and chroniclers
wrote in it (Firishta's *Tarikh* was composed at Bijapur), making
the Deccan a laboratory of the composite culture the Mughals later
adopted.
[/definition]

On the Bahmani side stand the domed, courtyard-less **Jami Masjid
of Gulbarga**, the **Madrasa of Mahmud Gawan** at Bidar, and the
royal tombs of Ashtur. Bijapur and Golconda perfected the line:
Ibrahim Rauza, the **Gol Gumbaz** (1656) with its whispering
gallery, and Hyderabad's **Charminar** (1591). Deccani painting
flourished as a bridge between Persian miniatures and the later
Mughal atelier.

[note]
Both kingdoms fell to their own contradictions — noble faction at
Bidar, courtly rivalry at Hampi — yet both left the Deccan a
shared civilisation: syncretic architecture, Dakhni letters,
diamond-fed markets, and a memory of power so strong that later
Marathas, Nizams, and nationalists all claimed its inheritance.
[/note]

